# Feature Request: Inline Audio Player for Audio File Attachments

## Summary

When a post contains a single audio file (MP3, WAV, M4A, FLAC, etc.), the Mattermost web app renders it as a generic file card (icon + filename). Clicking/tapping the card opens a full-screen preview modal to play the audio. This adds an unnecessary interaction step for a very common use case (sharing voice notes, short audio clips) and is inconsistent with the mobile client experience.

**Proposed change**: Render a compact inline audio player bar directly in the post for single audio files, matching the style of the mobile client's media player.

---

## Current Behavior (Web)

- Single audio file in a post → renders as a **file icon card** (same as PDFs, DOCs, etc.)
- Click/tap the card → opens a **full-screen file preview modal** with an audio player
- The user must dismiss the modal to continue reading the conversation
- **Desktop and mobile browsers get identical behavior** — there is no mobile-web-specific rendering logic

### Code Path (webapp v10.11.2)

In `webapp/channels/src/components/file_attachment/file_attachment_list.tsx`, the single-file branch only special-cases `FileTypes.IMAGE` and `FileTypes.SVG`:

```tsx
if (fileInfos.length === 1 && !fileInfos[0].archived) {
    const type = getPreviewType(fileInfos[0].extension);
    if (type === FileTypes.IMAGE || (type === FileTypes.SVG && enableSVGs)) {
        return <InlineImage ... />;
    }
    // audio, video, docs, etc. fall through to the generic file list below
}
// → renders <FileAttachmentIcon /> cards → click opens FILE_PREVIEW_MODAL
```

There is no `FileTypes.AUDIO` branch, so audio files are treated the same as any other non-image file.

---

## Desired Behavior

Render a **compact inline audio player** directly in the post for single audio files:

```
   ← 8px gap to surrounding content
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  voice_note_20260823.mp3                        1.2 MB  │
│  (▶)  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━●━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  3:26  (⬇) │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   ← 8px gap to surrounding content
```

| Element | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Filename row | Full filename above the player (truncated with ellipsis when too long, full name in `title` tooltip) + file size on the right (KB/MB/GB) |
| Play/Pause button | Circular button, toggles between ▶ and ⏸ |
| Seek bar | Clickable/draggable progress track with elapsed (blue) / remaining (gray) segments and a circular thumb |
| Time label | `m:ss` — shows current time while playing, total duration when idle |
| Download button | Downloads the original file |

- Max width ~480px, rounded border, matches post content width
- Works identically on desktop and mobile web (no user-agent detection needed)
- Multiple audio files (or audio mixed with other file types) → keep existing file card behavior; only **single-audio** posts get the inline player

---

## Rationale

1. **Mobile client parity**: The mobile app shows a compact media player bar for audio. The web app should match.
2. **Reduced interaction**: Playing a voice note currently requires 2 taps (open card → play in modal). With an inline player, it's 1 tap.
3. **Context preservation**: The full-screen modal hides the surrounding conversation. An inline player keeps the user in the thread.
4. **Low implementation cost**: The audio file URL (`/files/<id>`) and download URL (`/files/<id>?download=1`) are already available via existing helpers (`getFileRoute`, `getDownloadRoute`).

---

## Proof of Concept

A working proof of concept was implemented by patching the production webpack bundle (`2820.*.js`) of Mattermost Team Edition v10.11.2. The patch:

1. Injects a custom React component (`AudioPlayerBar`) into the `FileAttachmentList` module scope
2. Adds a `FileTypes.AUDIO` branch to the single-file rendering logic, before the fallback to file cards

The PoC was verified on both desktop (Chrome) and mobile (iOS Safari, Android Chrome) browsers.

### Component (simplified from PoC)

```tsx
function AudioPlayerBar({ file }: { file: FileInfo }) {
    const audioRef = useRef<HTMLAudioElement>(null);
    const trackRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
    const [state, setState] = useState({ playing: false, cur: 0, dur: 0 });

    // ⚠️ Use functional state updates to avoid stale closures.
    // The timeupdate/loadedmetadata handlers are registered once on mount
    // and capture the initial state values. Using setState(prev => ...)
    // ensures each update merges onto the LATEST state.
    const setPlaying = (v: boolean) =>
        setState(p => ({ ...p, playing: v }));
    const setCur = (v: number) =>
        setState(p => ({ ...p, cur: v }));
    const setDur = (v: number) =>
        setState(p => ({ ...p, dur: v }));

    useEffect(() => {
        const a = audioRef.current;
        if (!a) return;
        const onTime = () => setCur(a.currentTime);
        const onMeta = () => setDur(a.duration || 0);
        const onEnd = () => { setPlaying(false); setCur(0); };
        a.addEventListener('timeupdate', onTime);
        a.addEventListener('loadedmetadata', onMeta);
        a.addEventListener('ended', onEnd);
        return () => {
            a.removeEventListener('timeupdate', onTime);
            a.removeEventListener('loadedmetadata', onMeta);
            a.removeEventListener('ended', onEnd);
        };
    }, []);

    const toggle = () => {
        const a = audioRef.current;
        if (!a) return;
        if (a.paused) { a.play(); setPlaying(true); }
        else { a.pause(); setPlaying(false); }
    };

    const onSeek = (ev: React.MouseEvent) => {
        const a = audioRef.current;
        if (!a || !state.dur) return;
        const r = trackRef.current!.getBoundingClientRect();
        const f = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (ev.clientX - r.left) / r.width));
        a.currentTime = f * state.dur;
        setCur(f * state.dur);
    };

    const fmt = (t: number) => {
        if (!isFinite(t) || t < 0) t = 0;
        const m = Math.floor(t / 60);
        const s = Math.floor(t % 60);
        return `${m}:${s < 10 ? '0' : ''}${s}`;
    };

    const fmtSize = (b?: number) => {
        if (!b || b <= 0) return '';
        const units = ['B', 'KB', 'MB', 'GB'];
        let i = 0, v = b;
        while (v >= 1024 && i < units.length - 1) { v /= 1024; i++; }
        return `${i === 0 ? Math.round(v) : v.toFixed(1)} ${units[i]}`;
    };

    const pct = state.dur ? Math.min(100, (state.cur / state.dur) * 100) : 0;
    const url = getFileRoute(file.id);

    return (
        <div className="audio-player-bar" style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: 6, margin: '8px 0' }}>
            <audio ref={audioRef} src={url} preload="metadata" style={{ display: 'none' }} />
            {/* Filename + size row, above the player controls */}
            <div className="audio-player-bar__file" style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 8, minWidth: 0 }}>
                <span title={file.name} style={{ flex: 1, minWidth: 0, overflow: 'hidden', textOverflow: 'ellipsis', whiteSpace: 'nowrap' }}>{file.name}</span>
                <span style={{ flexShrink: 0, opacity: 0.6 }}>{fmtSize(file.size)}</span>
            </div>
            <div className="audio-player-bar__controls" style={{ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: 12, minWidth: 0 }}>
            <button onClick={toggle} aria-label={state.playing ? 'Pause' : 'Play'}>
                {state.playing ? <PauseIcon /> : <PlayIcon />}
            </button>
            <div ref={trackRef} onClick={onSeek} role="slider" aria-valuenow={Math.round(pct)}>
                <div className="track-elapsed" style={{ width: `${pct}%` }} />
                <div className="track-thumb" style={{ left: `${pct}%` }} />
            </div>
            <span className="time-label">{fmt(state.cur > 0 ? state.cur : state.dur)}</span>
            <a href={url} download={file.name} aria-label="Download">
                <DownloadIcon />
            </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    );
}
```

### Integration Point

In `file_attachment_list.tsx`, add the audio branch after the image branch:

```tsx
if (fileInfos.length === 1 && !fileInfos[0].archived) {
    const type = getPreviewType(fileInfos[0].extension);
    if (type === FileTypes.IMAGE || (type === FileTypes.SVG && enableSVGs)) {
        return <InlineImage ... />;
    }
    // NEW: inline audio player for single audio files
    if (type === FileTypes.AUDIO) {
        return <AudioPlayerBar file={fileInfos[0]} />;
    }
}
```

---

## Implementation Notes for a Source-Level PR

1. **Design system**: Use Mattermost's theme colors (`theme.centerChannelColor`, `theme.buttonBg`, etc.) and styled-components instead of hardcoded hex values.
2. **Accessibility**: Add `aria-label`, `role="slider"`, keyboard support (Space to play/pause, arrow keys to seek).
3. **i18n**: Wrap all user-facing text in `intl.formatMessage`.
4. **Styling**: Add a `.scss` file for the player bar, following the existing `file_attachment` styles.
5. **Testing**: Add a unit test for the `FileTypes.AUDIO` branch in `file_attachment_list.test.tsx`.
6. **Mobile web**: The component should work on mobile browsers without UA detection. Test on iOS Safari and Android Chrome.
7. **Multiple audio files**: When a post has multiple audio files (or audio + other files), keep the existing file card behavior. Only single-audio posts get the inline player.
8. **Audio types covered**: mp3, wav, wma, m4a, flac, aac, ogg, m4r (per `AUDIO_TYPES` in the webapp source).

---

## Known Pitfalls (from PoC)

- **React stale closure**: The `timeupdate` / `loadedmetadata` event handlers are registered once in a `useEffect([], ...)`. If you use `setState({ playing: playing, cur: v })` with the captured `playing` variable, every `timeupdate` will reset `playing` back to its initial value (`false`), causing the play/pause icon to flicker (shows pause for a frame, then reverts to play). **Always use functional updates**: `setState(prev => ({ ...prev, cur: v }))`.
- **Duration is 0 until `loadedmetadata`**: The seek bar percentage must handle `dur === 0` (render at 0% until metadata loads).
- **Cache busting**: Webpack chunk files are cached for 1 year (`Cache-Control: max-age=31556926`). After deploying a change, users must hard-refresh or use a private window to see the update.

---

## Environment (PoC)

- Mattermost Team Edition v10.11.2 (Docker)
- Web app bundle: `2820.448e420361c25075e45a.js` (webpack chunk, module 1754 = `FileAttachmentList`)
- Verified on: desktop Chrome, iOS Safari, Android Chrome

---

## Screenshots

**Mobile client** — the compact inline audio player bar this request asks to bring to the web app:

